{"tool_name":"convert-matrix","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"convert-matrix","home":"http://rsat.eu/","summary":"Inter-conversions between various formats of position-specific scoring matrices (PSSM). The program also performs a statistical analysis of the original matrix to provide different position-specific scores (weight, frequencies, information contents), general statistics (marginal frequencies, total information content), and synthetic descriptions (strict and degenerate consensus).","addition_date":"2016-03-26T09:59:12Z","last_update_date":"2019-06-19T13:43:40Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Web application","Web service"],"version":["1"],"collections":["Regulatory Sequence Analysis Tools (RSAT)"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:convert-matrix","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"Jacques.van-Helden@univ-amu.fr","name":"Jacques van Helden","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0002-8799-8584","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"Jacques.van-Helden@univ-amu.fr","name":"Jacques van Helden","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0002-8799-8584","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"RSAT","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://rsat.eu/"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence motif (nucleic acid)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2070"},"format":[{"term":"Sequence motif format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2068"}]}],"note":"Sequence motif conversion\nPSSM conversion \nPSSM statistics TRANSFAC-motif, JASPAR-motif, tab-motif, AlignACE-motif, cis-bp-motif, cluster-buster-motif, consensus-motif,  homer-motif, meme-motif, motifsampler-motif, stamp-motif, yeastract-motif TRANSFAC-motif, cluster-buster-motif, jaspar-motif, patser-motif, stamp-motif, tab-motif, tomtom-motif","operation":[{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2403"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence motif (nucleic acid)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2070"},"format":[{"term":"Sequence motif format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2068"}]}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"convert-matrix","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"jvanheld","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkn304","metadata":{"abstract":"The regulatory sequence analysis tools (RSAT, http://rsat.ulb.ac.be/rsat/) is a software suite that integrates a wide collection of modular tools for the detection of cis-regulatory elements in genome sequences. The suite includes programs for sequence retrieval, pattern discovery, phylogenetic footprint detection, pattern matching, genome scanning and feature map drawing. Random controls can be performed with random gene selections or by generating random sequences according to a variety of background models (Bernoulli, Markov). Beyond the original word-based pattern-discovery tools (oligo-analysis and dyad-analysis), we recently added a battery of tools for matrix-based detection of cis-acting elements, with some original features (adaptive background models, Markov-chain estimation of P-values) that do not exist in other matrix-based scanning tools. The web server offers an intuitive interface, where each program can be accessed either separately or connected to the other tools. In addition, the tools are now available as web services, enabling their integration in programmatic workflows. Genomes are regularly updated from various genome repositories (NCBI and EnsEMBL) and 682 organisms are currently supported. Since 1998, the tools have been used by several hundreds of researchers from all over the world. Several predictions made with RSAT were validated experimentally and published.","authors":[{"name":"Brohee S."},{"name":"Defrance M."},{"name":"Janky R."},{"name":"Sand O."},{"name":"Thomas-Chollier M."},{"name":"Turatsinze J.V."},{"name":"Vervisch E."},{"name":"van Helden J."}],"citationCount":206,"date":"2008-01-01